<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Natukhaj</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-governing Circassian people of the Black Sea coast around Anapa and Novorossiysk, among the first to fall to Russia in the 19th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Natukhaj were one of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a> groups of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/circassia">Circassia</a>, living in the coastal hills between the lower Kuban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anapa">Anapa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsemes-bay">Tsemes Bay</a>. They appear as a distinct community in Ottoman and Russian sources from the 17th and 18th centuries; earlier history is not recorded separately.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shapsugs">Shapsugs</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abadzekhs">Abadzekhs</a>, the Natukhaj overthrew the authority of their princes and nobles in the late 18th century and governed through assemblies of elders and free households. They farmed, kept livestock and traded at the Ottoman fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anapa">Anapa</a>, built in 1781, through which slaves, honey and hides passed to Ottoman ports.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anapa">Anapa</a> in 1828 and built coastal forts on Natukhaj land, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novorossiysk">Novorossiysk</a> in 1838. After a long resistance, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> completed its conquest in 1864, and nearly all Natukhaj were expelled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/natukhaj.jpg" alt="Natukhaj" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Daseyn · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Digital_Reconstruction_of_a_Natukhaj_flag,_Yellow_seal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Adyghe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Natukhai, Natukhay</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=natukhaj" aria-label="Natukhaj on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5619 228 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Natukhaj lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5955 888h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5939 484h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5939 484h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=natukhaj">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

